The `IOException` caught in `getEsqlQueryTask` is silently swallowed (returns `null`). While `AsyncTaskIndexService.getTask` performing an I/O operation that throws is unlikely, silently discarding the exception makes debugging harder if it does occur. A `logger.debug` or `logger.warn` with the exception attached would aid diagnosis without changing behavior:
```java
} catch (IOException e) {
    logger.debug("Failed to look up async task {}", asyncId, e);
    return null;
}
```

